  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1030 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nFriday begins with the upper high relocating from the South Plains \nto western New Mexico which should allow for a precious one or two \ndegree decline in high temps compared to Thursday. Some of this tiny \ncooling can be attributed to an uptick in dewpoints through the \nafternoon on breezier southerly winds ahead of a surface trough. \nThis trough is forecast to run from eastern NM to the TX Panhandle \nby midday, and when combined with a weak shortwave trough in NW flow \nand strong surface heating we expect storms by Friday afternoon and \nespecially the evening across much of our western and northern \nzones. Rather anemic northwest flow around 10 knots won't do much\nto steer these storms southward, but strong outflows should \nfoster some redevelopment away from the surface trough. Localized \nsevere winds, blowing dust, and heavy rain are all on the table \nbefore storms fade overnight. Any residual outflow boundaries or \nlingering MCVs in light NNE flow aloft could spur additional \nstorms by Saturday afternoon mainly on the Caprock, but this \npotential is much less than Friday owing to rising heights and \nsubsidence from the upper high expanding our way. Although still \nhot, high temps for Saturday should decline to their lowest \nreadings in days provided some high clouds remain in the area from\novernight storms. Thereafter, the story turns more dire as the \nupper high shifts squarely over the TX Panhandle through early \nnext week complete with hotter temps, including a likely encore of\nHeat Advisories off the Caprock.",
